To provide each test with a ready-to-use page object without boilerplate, you can extend the base test by providing fixtures. 

As shown in the `test-fixtures.md` source, you can declare the types of your fixtures (e.g., `type MyFixtures = { todoPage: TodoPage; settingsPage: SettingsPage; };`) and use `test.extend` to set up the page objects. For example:

```typescript
export const test = base.extend<MyFixtures>({
  todoPage: async ({ page }, use) => {
    const todoPage = new TodoPage(page);
    await todoPage.goto();
    // ... additional setup
    await use(todoPage);
    // ... cleanup
  },
  settingsPage: async ({ page }, use) => {
    await use(new SettingsPage(page));
  },
});
```

This new `test` object can be used in multiple test files, and each will receive the fixtures automatically.

